ENGLISH 96

&
\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% TRANSFORMATION TMoveTopic
< m1: I1::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
                [ I2:shiftrel/T1,
                  I3:mu1
                ]
>

< m : I1::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
                [ I2:leftdislocrel/T1,
                  I3:mu1
                ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: CLAUSErec1.finiteness = finite AND
           CLAUSErec1.deixis <> omegadeixis AND
           CLAUSErec1.superdeixis = omegadeixis
       I2: T1.CAT IN [NP, ADVP, PREPP] WITH T1.REC.mood = nowh
           (* more restrictive conditions will be needed *)
       I3: NOT EXIST (mu1, [shiftrel/..]) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TMoveTopic
\item[* relevant examples:] The house the man did please, the door the woman 
did paint green
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_TopicCleft
\item[* date of creation:] 4-OCT-1989 15:25:02  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to move a topicalized element in a main sentence to 
leftdislocrel position, so that it won't trigger inversion anymore (which it 
does if it stays in shiftrel)
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) More severe 
restrictions will have to be formulated on the elements for which this rule 
holds: 
constructions like {\em No sooner than, hardly\/} etc.\ do trigger inversion 
and thus should not go with this rule. \\
In this rule class rules will also be formulated to deal with clefting: the 
house the man pleased $\rightarrow$ it was the house that pleased the man.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
